  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "Intro to Web Lab",
      "name": "ISTE: Inside a CodeAI Classroom",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "Step into a mock classroom and experience how AI can support learning without becoming the center of the lesson. In this interactive session, participants will move through a compressed student experience built around three classroom activities: exploring how context shapes AI output, examining how patterns can reinforce assumptions or bias, and creating a digital artifact with intentional AI support.\n\nThe session demonstrates that AI does not change what good teaching and learning looks like. Participants will engage in unplugged learning, inquiry, communication, collaboration, choice, differentiation, and reflection while using AI as part of the learning environment. AI is present throughout, but it is never the point. The point is what students are doing with their thinking while AI is in the room.\n\nParticipants begin with a no-device warm-up that shows how humans and AI both rely on patterns and context. They then explore how AI-generated images can reflect assumptions about audience, culture, and “default” perspectives. Finally, participants utilize the AI Tutor along with content, wireframes, and style guides to create or customize a digital artifact such as a webpage, magazine cover, or guide.\n\nThe Pilot / Co-Pilot / Autopilot framework is introduced as a practical way to help students describe their relationship with AI during creative work: when they are leading, when they are collaborating, and when they may be letting AI take over. Participants leave having experienced a pedagogy-first CodeAI classroom activity, with clear connections to AI Discoveries and AI Foundations.",
        "student_overview": "In this session, you will explore how AI can support your learning, creativity, and decision-making without taking over the work. You will move through three activities that show how AI responds to context, how patterns can sometimes lead to assumptions or bias, and how you can use AI intentionally to create something with a clear purpose.\n\nYou will start by exploring how people make predictions from patterns and how adding more context changes what someone — or an AI system — might produce. Then you will use AI to generate images for a specific audience and look closely at what the results reveal about defaults, assumptions, and missing perspectives.\n\nFinally, you will use AI Tutor to create or customize a digital artifact, such as a webpage, magazine cover, or guide. You will make choices about your audience, purpose, content, and design while deciding how AI should support your work.\n\nThroughout the session, you will use the Pilot / Co-Pilot / Autopilot framework to reflect on your relationship with AI. Are you leading the work? Are you collaborating with AI? Or are you letting AI take over? By the end, you will have practiced using AI as a support for your own thinking, creativity, and judgment.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Intro to Web Lab",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "iste-2026"
      }
    }
  ],
