  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "d9e0dc5f-10e9-4779-bff6-9582b122f350",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This self-paced lesson introduces students to building webpages in Web Lab by working with AI tutor as an AI assistant and collaborative partner. The progression has three parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** Students explore how prompting, context, and small changes can shape AI outputs. By testing an AI image tool and changing one variable at a time, they see that AI responses are not neutral; they reflect patterns from training data, including bias and assumptions. The goal is to help students approach AI as investigators who test, question, and evaluate outputs rather than passively accepting them.\n- **Part 2:** Students build something real, fast. The goal is to give them a page they care about before the Essential Question is introduced. Expect students to spend between 20-30 minutes just in this first few levels as they get familiar with Web Lab and interacting with AI tutor, and spend time writing their initial vision and tweaking the first draft of their website.\n- **Part 3:** Students can further develop HTML and CSS conceptual understanding through investigation, prompting, and debugging challenges that mirror real design problems.Students explore writng HTML or CSS themselves, and AI Tutor is guiding so students can learn the foundational concepts.  \n\n> ⚠️ **Make sure AI Tutor is enabled:** AI Tutor is a required part of this lesson. If you plan on teaching it with your students, make sure you follow these steps:\n> 1) [Become a verified teacher](https://support.code.org/hc/en-us/articles/115001550131-How-to-Become-a-Verified-Teacher)\n> 2) [Assign the course to a section](https://support.code.org/hc/en-us/articles/202114633-Creating-Accounts-for-Students-and-Adding-Them-to-Your-Section)\n> 3) [Make sure AI Tools are enabled for that section](https://support.code.org/hc/en-us/articles/42550900593677-AI-Settings-Managing-Student-Access-to-AI-Tools)",
        "name": "Lesson Overview"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d9e0dc5f-10e9-4779-bff6-9582b122f350",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0069a833-fb03-4080-bc6c-0657412d6527"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0165e8be-ca2c-42f9-a1d9-9eaff768545e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Students learn that the quality of what they get from AI depends on the quality of what they ask, and that prompting is a skill they can build through experimentation. Working with an AI image tool, they practice systematic testing: changing one variable at a time to see how output shifts. \n\nAdditionally, through generating images, students see that AI does not invent neutral answers, it reproduces patterns from training data, including narrow ideas about something as ordinary as a \"healthy meal.\" The purpose is for students to recognize bias as a predictable result of how AI learns, not a random glitch, and to understand that unquestioned outputs can quietly spread those assumptions. \n\nThe bigger purpose is to establish the mindset that anchors the whole unit, that students are here to test and question AI rather than passively accept it. This first taste of control helps them see themselves as investigators from day one.\n\n| Level | Title | Description |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 2 | Introduction to Prompting | Students interact with a slideshow. They learn the importance of providing details when prompting an AI Chat tool. |\n| 3 | Exploring Context | Students select one or more activities to experiment and discover what kinds of prompts get the best results. |\n| 4 | Details Matter | Students explore how many details are necessary to recreate a given image. |\n| 5 | Create an Image for a Healthy Meal | Students explore how many details are necessary to recreate a given image. |\n| 6 | Understanding AI Models | Students explore how AI models progress from training to pattern recognition and then ouput |"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0165e8be-ca2c-42f9-a1d9-9eaff768545e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "71f15895-5007-4852-8fd7-5623429210e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa39eacc-140e-44d7-a287-0fb393469929",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Part 1: Thinking Critically About AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa39eacc-140e-44d7-a287-0fb393469929",
        "lesson_activity.key": "71f15895-5007-4852-8fd7-5623429210e1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "55b081a1-a72c-44ba-a9d2-4a6c0f715f73",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Students build something real, fast. The goal is to give them a page they care about before the Essential Question is introduced. Expect students to spend between 20-30 minutes just in this first few levels as they get familiar with Web Lab and interacting with AI tutor, and spend time writing their initial vision and tweaking the first draft of their website.\n\n| Level | Title | Description |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 8  | Introduction to Designing and Building | Students learn about the roles of developers and are introduced to key elements: audience, content, struture, and style.  |\n| 9 | Exploring Web Lab | Students make their first interaction with AI tutor using a pre-built otter webpage. They highlight an example prompt, send it to AI Tutor, and use the Accept/Reject mechanic for the first time. |\n| 10-11 | Developing Content | Students complete a `content.md` template for a topic of their own choosing, then prompt AI Tutor to provide feedback. |\n| 12-13 | Building the Structure | Students select a wireframe for the structure of their website and prompt the AI Tutor to generate an HTML page from it. They see the power of AI, but also the gaps between their vision and what's on the page |\n| 14-15 |  Adding Your Style | Students complete a `style.md` template to define the look and feel of the webpage. Then use AI Tutor to bring that style to the webpage. |\n| 16 | Reflection | Students return to the question of the day, _\"Who is in control, you or the AI?\"_ |"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "55b081a1-a72c-44ba-a9d2-4a6c0f715f73",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7fafc77d-a206-4995-93c9-c6aa282ae635"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "05b512fc-97c8-4010-9f4b-e361d4dd14bb",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Part 2: Designing and Building with AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "05b512fc-97c8-4010-9f4b-e361d4dd14bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7fafc77d-a206-4995-93c9-c6aa282ae635"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8cc0f871-3857-4e31-92de-b8f58693c7cb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this reflection, you will think about the role AI played in your work and the role you played as the human decision-maker. Sometimes you may act as the pilot by setting the direction and making the key choices. Sometimes AI may act as a co-pilot by suggesting ideas, giving feedback, or helping carry out your plan. At other times, AI may feel more like autopilot, doing a larger part of the work from the flight plan you provided. Responsible AI use means noticing which role you are in, staying engaged, checking the results, and being ready to adjust or take control when needed.",
        "progression_name": "Reflection: Working with AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8cc0f871-3857-4e31-92de-b8f58693c7cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7392b431-1262-48a0-8602-9747059b4aa1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "446930f5-d4a3-4d47-9e59-efda461aa399",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this section, you can continue exploring how AI can support your learning in Web Lab. You will choose HTML or CSS features to investigate, ask AI Tutor to explain how they work, and then use more specific vocabulary to improve a webpage. You may also work through prompting puzzles where you compare a goal, an AI-generated result, and the gap between them. The focus is not just getting AI to make changes, but understanding what changed and why.\n\n| Level | Title | Description |\n| :---- | :---- | :---- |\n| 21 | Inspecting Web Pages | Students use the Tag Inspector to hover over elements of the otter example site and identify the HTML tags and CSS rules responsible for each element. They then ask AI Tutor to explain at least one element they found. |\n| 22 | HTML Choice Levels | Students choose one HTML feature to investigate (headings, lists, images, paragraphs & line breaks, or sections). They ask AI Tutor how it works, then prompt AI to add new content to an example page using specific vocabulary. |\n| 23 | CSS Choice Levels | Students choose one CSS feature to investigate (color, font-family, border, text styles, or spacing). Same pattern as Level 7: ask AI Tutor how it works, then prompt AI to apply it to an example page. |\n| 24 | Prompting Puzzles | Students receive someone else's raw text content and a non-technical vision description with a screenshot. They prompt AI Tutor to add HTML structure and CSS style, iterating until their result is close to the target. |\n| 25 | Debug the Gap | Students see someone else's `vision.md` alongside the AI-generated output that didn't match it. They diagnose the mismatch and choose how to fix it \\- by updating the vision, prompting AI Tutor directly, or both. |\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "446930f5-d4a3-4d47-9e59-efda461aa399",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8974db3-a410-4d1e-8d16-948ccceb5421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a709fc14-854c-4ed4-b84e-4feb7cb338d9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Part 3: Independent Exploration"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a709fc14-854c-4ed4-b84e-4feb7cb338d9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a8974db3-a410-4d1e-8d16-948ccceb5421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "906c63a9-fc57-4a19-a78c-943b9a4c2b0c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "[**AI Foundations**](https://studio.code.org/courses/artificial-intelligence-foundations) introduces students to the core principles of computer science and the growing role of artificial intelligence in everyday life. Through hands-on programming, visual problem solving, and critical analysis, students explore how computing systems work, how data powers AI, and how intelligent tools make decisions. In addition, students design and build interactive web apps using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript while learning to collaborate thoughtfully with AI through design, coding, and project work.",
        "name": "What Comes Next?: Explore More AI Curricula"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "906c63a9-fc57-4a19-a78c-943b9a4c2b0c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f8e4621-1a22-4ae0-bf73-b7556954895e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e092cff9-a1f1-4eb9-8f16-5d2696b14421",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "[**AI Discoveries**](https://studio.code.org/courses/ai-discoveries-2026) is an introductory, classroom-based course appropriate for 6–8th grade students. The course aims to empower students to create authentic artifacts and engage with computer science and AI as media for creativity, communication, problem solving, and exploration. AI Discoveries (AID) takes a wide lens on computer science by covering topics such as programming, web development, design, and data, while placing AI literacy and computational thinking at the center of the experience. "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e092cff9-a1f1-4eb9-8f16-5d2696b14421",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f8e4621-1a22-4ae0-bf73-b7556954895e"
      }
    }
  ],
